@@ -304,10 +304,15 @@ Note 2: Master–vassal(s) changes are intended to strengthen overall AI play by
 - **Avoid excessively watery capitals:** if fewer than **12** "good starter BFC tiles" (land + coastal bonus tiles, excluding the city tile), apply a strong penalty so the capital doesn’t start surrounded by mostly low-yield coast. Other cities may still take such sites later. See [KI#44](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#44---enhanced-makeencourage-ai-settlers-walk-away-from-bad-starting-sites-in-this-case-i-mean-but).
 - **Additional tweaks:** value fresh water more (logic refactored/merged), value settling on **hill-plains** much more, and disable legacy "anti-crowd" biases so the choice stays yield-driven.
 - (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 5940+) **First-city scoring**: value usable food bonuses, fresh-water city tiles, and river support more for the capital than for ordinary later city sites, because early capital growth snowballs into faster tile access, worker/settler production, whipping, and specialists. See [KI#144](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#144---fixed-base-advciv-issue-and-advciv-sas-settler-free-window-follow-up-ai-settlers-sometimes-do-not-move-away-from-a-high-bad-plot-count-start-eg-high-non-bonus-tundra-and-plains-they-now-scout-and-hunt-for-better-not-very-bad-sites-and-no-longer-stop-at-first-good-enough-site-but-instead-now-rerun-evaluate-city-site-again-on-newly-visible-plots-if-a-better-site-eg-more-foodriversfresh-water-exists-nearby-which-we-now-value-more-too-for-first-city-as-well).
+- (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 6008+) **Removed map-generation spacing bias from live first-settler choices:** keeping players' assigned starts far enough apart makes sense while generating the map, but after play begins their settlers should be free to found wherever they evaluate as best, whether closer to or farther from rivals. BBAI logging in save file 442 showed that Berlin's greener, resource-rich `(33,13)` site was valued higher than the `(35,11)` (**4569 to 3934**), but the old map-generation spacing penalties of **-1482 versus -480** reversed them to **3087 versus 3454**. Removing this major cause of missed strong city sites helped but did not complete the Berlin fix alone; the smaller bonus-scoring and scouting changes as of now below then let the AI settler fully reveal and normally rank the stronger site, ultimately choosing `(33,13)` successfully without a Berlin-specific rule. Old Base AdvCiv runtime behavior can optionally be restored (not recommended). See [KI#173](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#173---fixedimproved-base-advciv-issue-live-first-settler-scoring-reused-hidden-map-generation-surroundings).
+- (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 6008+) **Simplified bonus-improvement yield valuation:** disabled AdvCiv's obscure nonlinear `evaluateSpecialYields` aggregation after the new SAS valuation made it redundant and retaining both paths heavily overscored bonus-rich sites. The inherited path combined many hardcoded fixed-point weights, context branches, and another food modifier; it was difficult to understand and could only be tuned by editing and recompiling the DLL. In the save file 360 Karakorum BBAI test, tundra-heavy `(49,43)` with Pig, 3 Elephants, and 2 Fur scored **6934 overall** (**7037** after more tiles were revealed), including **5015 resource points** and **4239 from the old special-yield path**. It therefore incorrectly beat the nearby river/Grass area: for example, Pig + Maize `(52,40)` scored only **4836**. After simplification, `(49,43)` fell to **3835 overall**, including **2126 resource points** and **1350 from actual bonus-improvement yields**; it then lost to nearby Pig + Maize river Grassland `(50,40)` at **4502**, which the AI settler chose. The new capital has stronger food, river support, and greener long-term workable land instead of being pulled toward low-food Tundra by duplicate Elephants and Fur. Actual Food, Production, and Commerce added by bonus improvements are now valued and tunable only once through the existing simple SAS defines (e.g., explicitly for Tundra or Flood Plains). Note: Bonus plots also receive the same tunable terrain valuation as non-bonus plots, so e.g., Iron on Grass is as of now valued higher than Iron on Snow.
+- (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 6008+) **Made Base AdvCiv's inherited natural-yield weights tunable:** Self-Sustaining, One-Food-Short, and Default Food/Production/Commerce values are now exposed through SAS XML defines. These actual-yield weights currently overlap with explicit SAS terrain/feature preferences such as Grass, Tundra, and Flood Plains; this is acceptable for now because those SAS values act as strategic adjustments on top of real yields, but the overlap can be revisited later.
+- (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 6008+) **Removed Base AdvCiv's separate first-era Commerce boost from city-site scoring:** city sites persist into later eras, Commerce is already valued normally, and early Food and Production are at least as important because they drive growth, workers, settlers, defense, and military; the same fundamentals apply to Barbarian cities. Applying an extra Commerce value only while evaluating the first two cities in the first era added complexity without a coherent long-term settlement priority.
+- (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 6008+) **Similarly, removed Base AdvCiv's small Barbarian production bias from city-site scoring:** Barbarian cities still need Food for growth and Commerce for research/economy, while normal scoring already values Production and Barbarian military priorities are handled elsewhere. Slightly reducing Food and Commerce to favor Production did not justify a separate tile-yield valuation layer, especially for such minor values.
 - **Free window:** during the first ~75 turns at Normal speed (scaled by game speed), a "free to produce settler" period lets the capital grow before switching into chosen early strategy (offense, defense, etc.).
 - **First city logic:** settlers consider a larger no-penalty scouting radius for founding the capital (`CvUnitAI::AI_foundFirstCity`), and no longer auto-reject non-coastal/non-river sites if a worse "preferred" site exists. It’ll just pick the **best** overall site. See [KI#43](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#43---attemptingly-improvedenhanced-ai-settlers-for-the-first-city-found-ie-at-turn-0-if-i-am-not-mistaken-settling-too-soon-instead-of-digging-a-bit-for-better-sites-and-other-related-changes).
 - (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 5940+) **First-city roaming**: Since the first city is especially important, unlike later cities that can be more strategic or semi-decent resource grabs, starting settlers can now **roam briefly for a better first-city site when the current site is bad enough**. Settlers can spend the bounded early window to **move to visible good-enough sites**, do **safe adjacent scouting for better information**, or **wait if no safe scouting step exists**. (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 5976+) Starting-site checks use a shared **potential-food environment score** (using the same logic described under Low-food environments). Food-surplus plots offset poor plots, productive Grass Hills remain neutral, and no-yield peaks/ice receive an extra citizen-unworkable penalty because they are dead BFC slots. This keeps useful resources/coasts from looking poor while still moving away from bad tundra/plains/peak-heavy starts.
-- (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 5940+) **First-city recheck after scouting**: **instead of settling the first good-enough** capital site found **after leaving a very bad start**, the **settler now runs first-city evaluation again** on **nearby visible plots** including **now newly visible** ones first, like a human player would. Empirically, this helped choose even stronger nearby capital sites with more usable food bonuses and/or river support rather than stopping at the first merely-no-longer-bad site. As of now this applies only to the first city, because as of now only the first city is deemed most needing it critically, and later settlers usually know more of the local map so they would generally have a lower need to change route midway (may create other issues too maybe). See [KI#144](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#144---fixed-base-advciv-issue-and-advciv-sas-settler-free-window-follow-up-ai-settlers-sometimes-do-not-move-away-from-a-high-bad-plot-count-start-eg-high-non-bonus-tundra-and-plains-they-now-scout-and-hunt-for-better-not-very-bad-sites-and-no-longer-stop-at-first-good-enough-site-but-instead-now-rerun-evaluate-city-site-again-on-newly-visible-plots-if-a-better-site-eg-more-foodriversfresh-water-exists-nearby-which-we-now-value-more-too-for-first-city-as-well).
+- (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 5940+) **First-city recheck after scouting**: **instead of settling the first good-enough** capital site found **after leaving a very bad start**, the **settler now runs first-city evaluation again** on **nearby visible plots** including **now newly visible** ones first, like a human player would. Empirically, this helped choose even stronger nearby capital sites with more usable food bonuses and/or river support rather than stopping at the first merely-no-longer-bad site. (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 6008+) Recovery candidates must now have a fully revealed BFC before interrupting bounded scouting; when the scouting window ends, the settler rechecks revealed reachable sites instead of blindly founding wherever scouting stopped. This prevents partially known sites from prematurely ending information gathering and prevents the final scouting tile from becoming the capital by accident. As of now this applies only to the first city, because as of now only the first city is deemed most needing it critically, and later settlers usually know more of the local map so they would generally have a lower need to change route midway (may create other issues too maybe). See [KI#144](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#144---fixed-base-advciv-issue-and-advciv-sas-settler-free-window-follow-up-ai-settlers-sometimes-do-not-move-away-from-a-high-bad-plot-count-start-eg-high-non-bonus-tundra-and-plains-they-now-scout-and-hunt-for-better-not-very-bad-sites-and-no-longer-stop-at-first-good-enough-site-but-instead-now-rerun-evaluate-city-site-again-on-newly-visible-plots-if-a-better-site-eg-more-foodriversfresh-water-exists-nearby-which-we-now-value-more-too-for-first-city-as-well).
 - (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 5928+) **Naval-heavy-map coastal settling:** On naval-heavy (e.g., Archipelago) maps, settler AI site valuation now optionally add **extra valuation for coastal sites**, as of now especially for the **first city**. This helps early work boat, scouting/contact, trade, first galleys, Moai, or other water-focused buildings; e.g., Cuzco needed a high first-city coastal extra valuation to prefer a coastal Pig + Banana + 2-Sugar river site over a central 4-Sugar river grass site; and kept **moderate for other cities** than the capital after testing showed higher values could, for example, overpull Japan toward tundra coast instead of grass. See [KI#146](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#146---fixedenhanced-base-advciv-issue-of-ai-undervaluing-coastal-settling-on-naval-heavy-maps).
 - **Parking addressed (early game):** Reduced cases of settlers idling in the capital despite safe/urgent expansion, by updating `CvUnitAI::AI_found` to found sooner when conditions allow. In autoplay samples, Spain shifted from **1 city @T100** to **~2 by T60–70** and **~3 by T80–90** (settler production varies somehow, but we move much sooner after we have it now). See [KI#68](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs/README_Known_Issues.md#68---tremendously-improved-ai-having-a-settler-parked-from-turn---45-to-turn-100-and-still-didnt-found-any-city-fixedaddressed-in-cvunitaiai_found).

